@namespace Lumeo
@using System.ComponentModel
@using System.Globalization
@using Lumeo.GanttV3
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject Lumeo.Services.IComponentInteropService Interop
@inject Lumeo.Services.Localization.ILumeoLocalizer L
@attribute [EditorBrowsable(EditorBrowsableState.Never)]

@* GanttTimeline — the scrollable header + row canvas (design spec Phase 2, T2
   "read-only markup"). Renders the two-row grouped header from
   GanttScale.BuildHeaderRows-equivalent helpers (UpperRuns/LowerLabels),
   vertical grid column lines, the today marker, and one GanttBar per task via
   a vertical Virtualize<T>.

   Deliberately takes Tasks/ViewMode/RangeStart/RangeEnd as plain parameters
   instead of a GanttState reference: GanttState is still `internal` (Phase-3
   promotion, per its own remarks — "becomes public API once a component
   actually exposes a State parameter wired to it"), and this component's
   generated partial class is unavoidably `public` (the Razor compiler always
   emits an explicit `public` modifier for component classes — there is no
   supported directive to change it; see dotnet/aspnetcore#5516 and
   dotnet/razor#8715, both open feature requests). A public `GanttState State`
   parameter would either force GanttState public too (jumping the gun on
   Phase 3) or fail to compile (CS0053, a public member can't expose a
   less-accessible type) — so GanttChart (the shared-state owner) unpacks its
   GanttState into these primitives instead.

   [EditorBrowsable(Never)] keeps this working-name API out of consumer
   IntelliSense until the Phase-4 rename (fix-round follow-up). *@
<div class="@RootClass" style="@RootStyle" @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">
    @* Keyboard-navigation live region (arrow-key nav's own a11y requirement —
       what a screen-reader user hears after a focus move or a keyboard-driven
       schedule edit). Follows the SAME sr-only aria-live="polite" span idiom
       already established across this repo (Lumeo.Sortable's own
       _reorderAnnouncement, Lumeo.DataGrid's _columnReorderAnnouncement) —
       the Scheduler's first-party views (feat/scheduler-first-party) don't
       actually have an announcement precedent of their own to follow yet
       (that branch isn't merged here and has no aria-live region at all), so
       this follows the Sortable/DataGrid convention instead, the closest
       actual first-party precedent in this codebase. A single element at the
       ROOT (not nested inside the virtualized row canvas) so it's always
       present in the accessibility tree regardless of scroll/virtualization. *@
    <span aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" class="sr-only">@_keyboardAnnouncement</span>
    @* Two-row header (Codex round 2, P1 #3 — "sticky header still broken").
       Deliberately OUTSIDE the horizontal-scroll wrapper below: `position:
       sticky` resolves against the NEAREST ancestor that establishes a
       scroll container, and the row-canvas wrapper's own `overflow-x:auto`
       counts as one — EVEN THOUGH its height auto-fits its content and it
       therefore never has anything to scroll vertically — so the header
       kept sticking to THAT (never-actually-scrolling) wrapper instead of
       to GanttChart's shared OUTER (vertical) scroller, and scrolled away with
       everything else the moment the outer pane actually moved. The wave-1
       "overflow-y-visible" fix never worked: the CSS Overflow spec silently
       PROMOTES a sibling `overflow-y: visible` to `overflow-y: auto` the
       moment `overflow-x` is anything but `visible`/`clip` on the SAME box,
       so that wrapper was STILL a scroll container on both axes regardless.
       With the header moved here (no intervening scroll container between
       it and GanttChart's outer pane), it can no longer physically BE the same
       scrolling element as the row canvas below — HeaderInnerStyle's width
       spans every column same as before, and gantt-v3.js's
       registerHeaderScrollSync mirrors the canvas's scrollLeft onto it via
       a transform so the two stay column-aligned. *@
    <div class="lumeo-gantt-v3-header sticky top-0 z-10 overflow-hidden border-b border-border/60 bg-card" style="@HeaderOuterStyle">
        @* class="relative" on the headerInner (not the upper-row div below) is
           the today-dot's OWN positioning context — see the dot's own remarks
           near the bottom of this block for why it is a SIBLING of, not
           nested inside, the upper-row div: E2E's ReadV3UpperRunTexts locator
           (`.lumeo-gantt-v3-header > div > div:nth-child(1) > div`) reads
           EVERY direct child of the upper-row div as an upper-run label —
           nesting the dot there polluted that read with an extra
           empty-text-content entry (real bug, caught live: Header_label_runs_
           match_between_v2_and_v3(Year) expected zero upper runs, actual
           [""]). *@
        <div @ref="_headerInnerRef" class="relative" style="@HeaderInnerStyle">
            <div class="relative" style="height:20px;">
                @foreach (var run in UpperRuns)
                {
                    <div class="absolute truncate text-xs font-semibold text-foreground" style="@UpperRunStyle(run)">@run.Label</div>
                }
            </div>
            @* Bug fix (Codex round 4, P2 #9): this row's date columns are
               rendered in DOM order (earliest first) and rely on that DOM
               order to lay out left-to-right — but it's the ONLY part of the
               header using `display:flex` for layout (the upper run labels
               above, like every bar/grid-line, use `position:absolute; left:
               Xpx`, which is direction-agnostic). A flex container's default
               `flex-direction:row` reverses child ORDER under an inherited
               `dir="rtl"` (Lumeo's DirectionProvider), so under RTL this row
               alone would visually flip to latest-first while the
               grid-lines/bars/upper-labels stayed physically earliest-first,
               misaligning every column. `dir="ltr"` forces this ONE flex
               container's layout direction back to physical (DOM) order
               regardless of the page's surrounding direction — matching how
               every other coordinate in this canvas is already
               direction-agnostic (v3's date axis is never mirrored for RTL;
               see gantt-v3.js's own remarks on why only the SCROLL position
               needed RTL handling, not the layout itself). *@
            <div class="relative flex" dir="ltr" style="height:@((GanttScale.HeaderHeight - 20).ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))px;">
                @for (var i = 0; i < Units.Count; i++)
                {
                    var label = LowerLabels[i];
                    @* Today column accent label (design spec Phase 3, T2) —
                       IsTodayColumn(i) gates only the CSS class, same column
                       cell markup otherwise. data-off (styling-hooks audit,
                       ReUI parity) — presence-only, same IsOffDayCell gate the
                       canvas backdrop tint below already uses, so the header
                       cell and the canvas band directly beneath it can never
                       disagree about which columns are "off". *@
                    <div class="@ColumnCellClass(i)" style="@ColumnCellStyle" data-off="@(IsOffDayCell(i) ? string.Empty : null)">@label</div>
                }
            </div>

            @* Today header dot (design spec Phase 3, T2 — "accent header
               label + dot + full-column tint, replacing the line-only
               look"; see TodayColumnStyle's own remarks for the shared
               column-boundary math this and the row-canvas tint below
               both use). A SIBLING of the upper/lower-row divs (not nested in
               either — see the headerInner's own remarks above for why),
               positioned absolutely against headerInner's own new
               "relative" context. *@
            @if (TodayHighlight && TodayInRange)
            {
                @* Theme-token status-dot rounding (the calc(var(--radius)*N)
                   form), same convention as Badge.razor's own dot and
                   ToolCallCard's h-1.5/w-1.5 status dots — RadiusTokenGuardTests
                   pins the OTHER (hardcoded-circle) utility to an audited
                   semantic allowlist; a small status dot is thematic
                   roundness, not the Spinner/Avatar-class "circle IS the
                   semantic" exception. *@
                <div class="lumeo-gantt-v3-today-dot pointer-events-none absolute h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-[calc(var(--radius)*2)] bg-primary" style="@TodayDotStyle"></div>
            }
        </div>
    </div>

    @* Row-canvas horizontal-scroll host — the ONLY scrolling element now (no
       sticky descendant left inside it, so its own overflow-y-auto side
       effect — see the header's remarks above — is harmless: nothing here
       needs to escape it via sticky positioning anymore). Coordinate origin
       is row 0's own top (no header allowance — the header lives outside
       this subtree entirely now), so GridLinesContainerStyle/TodayLineStyle/
       CanvasHeight and GanttArrowRouting's SourceEdge/TargetEdge all dropped
       their previous "+GanttScale.HeaderHeight" terms accordingly. *@
    <div class="@CanvasScrollClass" @ref="_scrollHostRef" data-gantt-v3-drag-origin="@_lastConfirmedDragOriginIso">
        <div class="relative" style="@CanvasStyle">
            @* Off-day column tint (design spec Phase 3, T2 — "Mark off days").
               Rendered BEHIND the grid lines/bars (background fill, painted
               first in DOM order = bottom of the stacking context here — none
               of these siblings set z-index, so paint order follows DOM
               order). Only rendered for day-granularity view modes — see
               SupportsOffDayMarking's own remarks for why coarser modes
               (Week/Month/Quarter/Year, whose OWN grid columns already span
               far more than a single day) are excluded. *@
            @if (MarkOffDays && SupportsOffDayMarking)
            {
                @for (var i = 0; i < Units.Count; i++)
                {
                    if (IsOffDayColumn(i))
                    {
                        @* data-off (styling-hooks audit, ReUI parity) — unconditional
                           here: this div only ever renders for a column IsOffDayColumn
                           already confirmed true (the @if above), unlike the header
                           cell's own data-off (every cell renders, off or not, so THAT
                           one still needs the conditional). *@
                        <div class="lumeo-gantt-v3-off-day pointer-events-none absolute inset-y-0 bg-muted/40" style="@OffDayColumnStyle(i)" data-off=""></div>
                    }
                }
            }

            @* Vertical grid lines, spanning the full row canvas height. *@
            <div class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0" style="@GridLinesContainerStyle">
                @for (var i = 0; i <= Units.Count; i++)
                {
                    <div class="absolute inset-y-0 border-s border-border/40" style="@GridLineStyle(i)"></div>
                }
            </div>

            @* Today column treatment (design spec Phase 3, T2 — "accent header
               label + dot + full-column tint (params: keep TodayHighlight as
               master switch), replacing the line-only look"). REPLACES the
               prior line-only `.lumeo-gantt-v3-today-line` — see
               TodayColumnStyle's own remarks for why this tint's left edge is
               STILL the exact same pixel this codebase's own ground-truth math
               (GanttDayModeMath) and v2's own today-line already agree on for
               every mode whose columns are exactly 1 day wide (Day/QuarterDay/
               HalfDay) — the parity/E2E position assertion this replaces did
               not need to weaken, only re-target its DOM selector.

               G8 (2026-08-10 shadcn alignment): the FULL-COLUMN tint is only
               meaningful when a rendered column IS a single day — the SAME
               "category error" SupportsOffDayMarking/IsDayGranularityMode
               already gates off-day shading against (a Week/Month/Quarter/Year
               column spans many days; tinting the WHOLE thing as "today" is
               precisely as wrong as tinting it "off day"). Day-granularity
               modes keep the exact tint above; coarser modes fall back to a
               precise 2px line at Today's own exact pixel (TodayX, not the
               floored TodayColumnLeft) — restoring v2's own line-only
               precision for exactly the modes where a whole-column band would
               mislead, while day-granularity modes keep the T2 upgrade
               unchanged (TodayColumnLeft == TodayX there anyway — see
               TodayColumnLeft's own remarks — so this is not a second,
               independently-drifting position formula, just a second RENDER
               of the same value pinned by GanttDayModeMath). *@
            @if (TodayHighlight && TodayInRange)
            {
                @if (IsDayGranularityMode)
                {
                    <div class="lumeo-gantt-v3-today-tint pointer-events-none absolute inset-y-0 bg-primary/10" style="@TodayColumnStyle"></div>
                }
                else
                {
                    <div class="lumeo-gantt-v3-today-line pointer-events-none absolute inset-y-0 border-s-2 border-primary" style="@TodayLineStyle"></div>
                }
            }

            @* Now indicator (design spec Phase 3, T2 — precise current-TIME
               line, DISTINCT from the today column above: only meaningful in
               sub-day (Hour-unit) modes, where a day itself spans many
               columns and "the exact current minute" is a real, renderable
               position — Day/Week/Month/Quarter/Year's columns are already
               coarser than "right now" could distinguish. Uses --color-info
               (existing Lumeo token) rather than --color-primary specifically
               so it reads as visually DISTINCT from the today column's own
               accent — no new CSS token needed. *@
            @if (NowInRange)
            {
                <div class="lumeo-gantt-v3-now-line pointer-events-none absolute inset-y-0 border-s border-info opacity-80" style="@NowLineStyle"></div>
            }

            @* Empty state (Codex round 3, P2 #4): v2 renders a centered "No
               tasks to display" text node when tasks.length === 0
               (gantt-v2.js:407-417); v3 previously rendered nothing at all —
               a blank grid with no explanation for why. Ported as a plain
               centered message rather than v2's SVG <text> (v3 has no SVG
               canvas to place it on), localized via the same Gantt.* resource
               family as every other Gantt string. Only shown when there are
               NO rows at all (a GroupHeader-only row list still has content
               worth showing). *@
            @if (EffectiveRows.Count == 0)
            {
                <div class="lumeo-gantt-v3-empty pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 flex items-center justify-center text-sm text-muted-foreground">@L["Gantt.NoTasksToDisplay"]</div>
            }

            @* Row canvas. *@
            <div class="relative" style="@RowsContainerStyle">
                <Virtualize Items="@RowItems" Context="row" ItemSize="@((float)GanttScale.RowHeight)">
                    @* Bug fix (Codex round 10 review, P2 #1): every item template
                       below renders ONLY absolutely-positioned content (GanttBar's
                       own wrapper, the group-header stripe) — neither establishes
                       any IN-FLOW box, so each materialized <Virtualize> item
                       contributed 0px to normal document flow. Virtualize's own
                       placeholder/spacer sizing (and, on some runtimes, its
                       IntersectionObserver-based "load more" triggering) depends on
                       rendered items actually occupying real, measurable space in
                       flow, not just on the ItemSize parameter used for spacer math
                       — a 0-height rendered window is a real mismatch between what
                       Virtualize assumes is on screen and what actually is. This
                       wrapper is plain (no position:relative/absolute of its own),
                       so it does NOT become a new containing block for the
                       absolutely-positioned content inside it — GanttBar/the
                       group-header stripe keep resolving their `position:absolute`
                       against the SAME ancestor as before (RowsContainerStyle's own
                       `.relative` div above), so visual geometry is byte-identical;
                       this only adds a correctly-sized IN-FLOW box alongside it. *@
                    @* Bug fix (Codex round 15 review, P2): this wrapper carried
                       no @key at all — a live task-set update (or a GroupBy-
                       driven reorder) that changes WHICH task occupies a given
                       row index let Blazor's diffing reuse the SAME <GanttBar>
                       component instance across renders for what is now a
                       DIFFERENT task, since without a key Blazor matches
                       children positionally. GanttBar owns its own JS-interop-
                       backed state (tooltip visibility, etc. — see GanttBar's
                       own remarks) that must reset per-task, not silently
                       survive onto whatever task now lands in that slot.
                       row.Task's own Id is the SAME stable per-task identity
                       GanttRowModel.ToggleKey already establishes for a task
                       row (see GanttVisibleRow's remarks); a group-header slot
                       has no Task at all, so it falls back to its row index —
                       stable enough since no GanttBar (the component actually
                       carrying reusable interop state) ever renders there.

                       Bug fix (Codex round 16 review, P2): the task-id branch
                       and the group-header fallback shared ONE flat string
                       namespace — a task whose own Id happened to literally BE
                       "group-0" (or any other value matching the synthetic
                       "group-{index}" pattern) could collide with a REAL
                       group-header row's key at that same index, since nothing
                       distinguished "a task whose id is the string group-0"
                       from "the synthetic key for row 0's own group header".
                       Prefixed both branches distinctly ("t:"/"g:") — the same
                       separate-namespace-via-prefix idiom
                       GanttRowModel.GroupToggleKey already established for the
                       identical class of collision risk between a task's own
                       Id and a group label in GanttState.Collapsed's shared
                       key set (see its own remarks: "a distinct namespace so a
                       task id can never collide with a group key"). *@
                    @* "hover:bg-accent/10 transition-colors" (design spec Phase 3,
                       T7 — "row hover states"): this wrapper is the ONLY
                       element that ALWAYS renders once per row regardless of
                       AllowCreate/ShowSummaryBars/etc (round-10's own remarks
                       above: it establishes no positioning context, but IS a
                       normal in-flow block box, so it already spans this
                       row's full rendered width by default block-level
                       sizing) — reusing it for row hover costs zero new DOM,
                       matching GanttTree.RowClass's own identical treatment
                       on the sibling pane. *@
                    @* G3 (2026-08-10 shadcn alignment): the canvas previously drew ONLY
                       vertical day grid lines (see GridLineStyle above); a multi-row chart
                       read as an unruled block past the row labels since GanttTree already
                       draws horizontal row separators (RowClass below) but the timeline
                       pane where the bars actually live did not. Reusing this SAME
                       always-rendered, in-flow row wrapper (round-10's own remarks above)
                       costs zero new DOM — border-b paints the separator at the row's own
                       bottom edge, unioned with the pointer-events-none vertical grid lines
                       already in this stacking context (paint order irrelevant since
                       neither sets z-index and they never overlap the SAME edge pixel
                       except at intersections, same as GanttTree's own row lines). Left at
                       border-border/50 to match the demo's row rule opacity; unifies with
                       GanttTree.RowClass's own /30 → /50 change below. *@
                    <div class="lumeo-gantt-v3-row-item hover:bg-accent/10 transition-colors border-b border-border/50" @key="@(row.Task is not null ? "t:" + row.Task.Id : $"g:{row.RowIndex}")" style="height:@(GanttScale.RowHeight)px;">
                        @if (row.Task is not null)
                        {
                            @* Phase 3, T1 — empty-track drag-create hit target
                               (Phase 2, T3's own feature), now rendered INSIDE
                               THIS SAME virtualized item instead of a separate
                               @foreach loop that used to render one such div
                               per EffectiveRows entry BEFORE this Virtualize
                               block regardless of scroll position — the bars
                               beside it were already virtualized (Codex round
                               3, P2 #1, pulled forward from Phase 3 T1; see
                               GanttV3StickyHeaderTests' own remarks), but this
                               track div was not, so a tall AllowCreate chart
                               still materialized all N of these on every
                               render. Only rendered at all when AllowCreate is
                               on AND not Readonly (Readonly wins
                               unconditionally — see AllowCreate's own remarks)
                               so the opt-in feature costs nothing (no extra
                               DOM, no extra hit-test surface) for every
                               existing v3 chart that never turns it on.
                               Rendered BEFORE GanttBar below — paint order
                               (later DOM wins, same stacking context —
                               RowsContainerStyle's own `.relative` ancestor;
                               this wrapper establishes no positioning context
                               of its own, per the round-10 remarks above) is
                               unchanged from the old sibling-loop's ordering,
                               so the bar still wins the hit-test over its own
                               row's track when the two overlap —
                               gantt-v3.js's onPointerDown only ever falls
                               through to '[data-gantt-row-track]' when
                               e.target.closest('[data-task-id]') found
                               nothing. "task:" + row.Task.Id mirrors RowKey's
                               own Task branch (see RowKey's remarks) —
                               GroupHeader rows never reach this branch (Task
                               is null there), so there is no risk of
                               duplicating the pair the group-header stripe
                               below already carries for its own row. *@
                            @if (AllowCreate && !Readonly)
                            {
                                @* touch-pan-y — see GanttBar.WrapperClass's own remarks on the
                                   identical fix ("Opt touch drag targets out of native
                                   horizontal panning"); unconditional here since this whole
                                   block is already gated on AllowCreate && !Readonly. *@
                                <div class="absolute touch-pan-y" data-gantt-row-track="true" data-row-key="@("task:" + row.Task.Id)" style="@RowTrackStyle(row.RowIndex)"></div>
                            }
                            @* data-selected is LEAF-ONLY (Codex review of the styling-hooks
                               PR, P2). A plain membership test disagrees with the tree for a
                               row that USED to be a leaf and became a parent on a later task
                               update: task replacement does not normalize SelectedIds, so the
                               stale id survives, while GanttSelectionModel derives a parent's
                               checkbox purely from its descendant leaves and can legitimately
                               show it unchecked — data-selected would then contradict the
                               checkbox on the very same row. *@
                            <GanttBar Task="row.Task" X="row.X" Width="row.Width" RowIndex="row.RowIndex"
                                      BarHeight="EffectiveBarHeight" Color="@row.Color" BarTemplate="BarTemplate"
                                      DragGhostTemplate="DragGhostTemplate"
                                      Readonly="@Readonly" OnTaskClick="OnTaskClick"
                                      SuppressPointerClick="@_dragConfirmed"
                                      Selected="@(!row.IsParent && SelectedIds.Contains(row.Task.Id))" Now="@EffectiveNow"
                                      FocusedTaskId="@EffectiveFocusedTaskId" OnKeyNavigation="HandleBarKeyNavigationAsync" />
                            @* Design spec Phase 3, T3 — a hierarchy-parent Task row (HasChildren)
                               renders BOTH its own GanttBar above (the task's own dates) AND this
                               envelope strip (its children's rolled-up dates) — see
                               GanttSummaryBar's own remarks for why the two never visually
                               collide (flush-bottom vs row-centered). row.Rollup is null for
                               every leaf row, so this simply doesn't render there. *@
                            @if (row.Rollup is { } taskRollup)
                            {
                                <GanttSummaryBar Rollup="taskRollup" X="row.SummaryX" Width="row.SummaryWidth"
                                                  RowIndex="row.RowIndex" SummaryTemplate="SummaryTemplate" />
                            }
                            @* Off-screen indicator chip (design spec Phase 3, T7). The
                               OUTER div is a plain position:absolute box spanning this
                               ROW's own full pixel extent (RowTrackStyle — same helper
                               the AllowCreate track div above already uses) purely to
                               give the INNER button's own position:sticky something to
                               stay CONSTRAINED within (sticky never escapes its nearest
                               block-level ancestor's box) — h-full + flex centering
                               replaces GanttScale.BarTop's usual top-math (which
                               position:sticky can't combine with a top offset the same
                               way position:absolute does) for vertical centering within
                               the row. The BUTTON is what's actually sticky — pinned to
                               the visible scroll edge PURELY VIA CSS, no per-frame JS or
                               Blazor work: only WHETHER it renders at all (row.Offscreen)
                               is driven by the rAF-throttled scroll report (see
                               VisibleTimelineWindow's own remarks), at the SAME
                               once-per-render cost class RowItems already pays for every
                               other per-row computed value. z-20 (matching the header's
                               z-10/the splitter's z-30) keeps it above bars/grid-lines
                               regardless of DOM order. *@
                            @if (row.Offscreen != GanttOffscreenDirection.None)
                            {
                                @* Bug fix (found empirically, live Chromium — position:sticky's
                                   "hold in place while scrolling toward it" behavior only
                                   engages when the element's own UNSTUCK flow position sits
                                   NEAR THE EDGE it sticks to: a sticky-end child whose natural
                                   flow position is at the row's own START (this flex wrapper's
                                   default justify-start) never approaches the end edge as the
                                   pane scrolls, so it never "catches" there — verified by
                                   reading the LIVE BoundingBoxAsync() of a rendered "after"
                                   chip, which landed at the row's START, not the viewport's end,
                                   despite computed style correctly showing position:sticky and a
                                   resolved inset-inline-end value. justify-end for the After
                                   direction gives it a natural position AT the row's own end
                                   (the far side of a TotalWidth-wide row), matching the "before"
                                   direction's own already-correct default (justify-start, natural
                                   position already at the row's start — the "before" chip never
                                   exhibited this bug because its sticky/natural edges already
                                   coincided by default). *@
                                <div class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-y-0 z-20 flex h-full items-center" style="@RowTrackStyle(row.RowIndex)">
                                    <div class="@(row.Offscreen == GanttOffscreenDirection.After ? "flex w-full justify-end" : "contents")">
                                        <button type="button"
                                                class="@OffscreenChipClass(row.Offscreen)"
                                                style="@OffscreenChipStyle(row.Offscreen)"
                                                data-gantt-offscreen-chip="@(row.Offscreen == GanttOffscreenDirection.Before ? "before" : "after")"
                                                aria-label="@(row.Offscreen == GanttOffscreenDirection.Before ? L["Gantt.ScrollToEarlierTask", row.Task.Name] : L["Gantt.ScrollToLaterTask", row.Task.Name])"
                                                @onclick="() => ScrollToBarAsync(row)">
                                            <SvgGlyph Svg="@(row.Offscreen == GanttOffscreenDirection.Before ? LumeoIcons.ChevronLeft : LumeoIcons.ChevronRight)" class="h-3 w-3" />
                                        </button>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
                            }
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            @* Group-header row (Codex round 2, P2 #7): before this fix a
                               GanttRowKind.GroupHeader slot rendered NOTHING here at all —
                               an empty gap in the row canvas — even though GanttTree (the
                               sibling pane) already shows a labeled summary row for the
                               SAME slot. v2 parity: gantt-v2.js:428-449 draws a full-width
                               translucent stripe (tokens.accent, 0.18 fill-opacity) plus an
                               uppercase, bold, muted-color label, left-padded 8px, vertically
                               centered in the row — ported below via Tailwind utilities +
                               the bg-accent/[0.18] arbitrary-opacity value matching v2's
                               literal 0.18 exactly. NOT a literal 1:1 port in every detail
                               (Codex round 3, Minor #8 — corrected wording): the label uses
                               Tailwind's tracking-wide (0.025em), not v2's literal
                               letter-spacing:0.04em — a deliberate, tiny typographic delta
                               (matching Lumeo's own type-scale token rather than hand-picking
                               an arbitrary value for a 1px-at-typical-sizes difference), not
                               a CSS-bundle-churning arbitrary tracking value.

                               Bug fix (Codex P2 finding, "Make group-header
                               tracks hittable"): a plain per-row
                               [data-gantt-row-track] div (rendered inside the
                               TASK branch above, for a Task row — see its own
                               remarks) would paint BELOW this stripe if a
                               GroupHeader row ever rendered one too —
                               gantt-v3.js's onPointerDown hit-tests via
                               e.target.closest('[data-gantt-row-track]'),
                               which a click on a fully-covered, hence
                               unreachable, div below could never satisfy, so
                               AllowCreate's drag-create path was unreachable
                               for any grouped row. Fixed by carrying the SAME
                               data-* pair directly on the stripe (gated on the
                               identical AllowCreate/Readonly condition the
                               task branch's own track div uses, so a chart
                               with the feature off still emits neither
                               attribute) — see GanttTimelineRow.GroupRowKey's
                               own remarks for where the key comes from.
                               Follow-up bug fix (E2E strict-mode violation —
                               two elements answering
                               '[data-gantt-row-track][data-row-key=...]' for
                               the SAME group row): a GroupHeader row's Task is
                               always null, so it never reaches the task
                               branch's own track div at all (see its remarks)
                               — this stripe is the row's ONLY element
                               carrying the pair, never a sibling duplicating
                               it. *@
                            @* touch-pan-y (Codex P2 finding "Opt touch drag targets out of
                               native horizontal panning" — see GanttBar.WrapperClass's own
                               remarks): gated on the SAME AllowCreate && !Readonly condition
                               as the data-gantt-row-track/data-row-key pair above — this
                               stripe is only ever an actual drag TARGET when both are true;
                               otherwise it's just a label, with no gesture to protect. *@
                            <div class="@GroupHeaderClass"
                                 style="@GroupHeaderStyle(row.RowIndex)"
                                 data-gantt-row-track="@(AllowCreate && !Readonly ? "true" : null)"
                                 data-row-key="@(AllowCreate && !Readonly ? row.GroupRowKey : null)">@row.Label</div>
                            @* Design spec Phase 3, T3 — a GroupHeader row never has its own
                               GanttBar (Task is always null), so this is the row's ONLY bar-like
                               content besides the label stripe above. *@
                            @if (row.Rollup is { } groupRollup)
                            {
                                <GanttSummaryBar Rollup="groupRollup" X="row.SummaryX" Width="row.SummaryWidth"
                                                  RowIndex="row.RowIndex" SummaryTemplate="SummaryTemplate" />
                            }
                        }
                    </div>
                </Virtualize>
            </div>

            @* Dependency-arrow overlay — see GanttArrowLayer's own remarks for why it
               recomputes geometry independently from the same EffectiveRows/GanttScale
               inputs rather than reaching into this component's RowItems. *@
            <GanttArrowLayer Rows="@EffectiveRows" ViewMode="@ViewMode" Origin="@Origin"
                             ColumnWidth="@EffectiveColumnWidth" BarHeight="@EffectiveBarHeight"
                             Width="@TotalWidth" Height="@CanvasHeight" VisibleRowRange="@_visibleRowRange" />

            @* Floating zoom control (design spec Phase 3, T7 — "+/- overlay in
               the canvas corner"). The OUTER div is a plain position:absolute
               box spanning the FULL canvas (inset-0 — a genuine, sized
               containing block for the sticky child, same reasoning as the
               off-screen chip's own outer wrapper above).

               flex justify-end items-end (bug fix, found empirically in live
               Chromium — see the off-screen chip's own identical remarks
               above for the full mechanism): position:sticky only "holds an
               element in place while scrolling toward it" when the
               element's UNSTUCK/natural flow position already sits near the
               edge it sticks to (exactly how the header's existing "sticky
               top-0" already works — the header's own unstuck position IS
               the top of a tall scrolling container, so top-sticky has
               something to catch onto). A sticky bottom-end child whose
               default (unstyled) flow position is the wrapper's own
               top-start corner never approaches the bottom-end edge as the
               canvas scrolls, so it never engages — confirmed by reading
               the LIVE BoundingBoxAsync() of a bare sticky child here,
               which landed at the wrapper's own top-start corner regardless
               of a correctly-resolved computed inset-inline-end/bottom
               value. justify-end/items-end gives the child a natural
               position AT the wrapper's own bottom-end corner instead
               (the wrapper spans the full TotalWidth/CanvasHeight canvas),
               so sticky now has a genuine "edge to hold onto" on both axes.

               pointer-events-none on the outer wrapper (never a click
               target itself) + pointer-events-auto on GanttZoomControl's
               own root (via Class) restores real clicks to the control
               alone. *@
            @if (ShowZoomControl)
            {
                <div class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 flex justify-end items-end">
                    <GanttZoomControl Class="pointer-events-auto sticky bottom-2 end-2 z-20" ViewMode="@ViewMode"
                                      ViewModeChanged="ViewModeChanged" ZoomLevels="@ZoomLevels" />
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@* Design spec Phase 3, T8 — bar context menu (right-click a bar opens a Lumeo
   ContextMenu at the pointer). Deliberately NOT composed from
   <ContextMenu>/<ContextMenuTrigger> — those render an extra "relative
   inline-block" wrapper div around whatever they enclose; wrapping the
   row-canvas markup above in one would insert a NEW positioned ancestor
   between the bars/arrows and the containing block they resolve their own
   position:absolute against today, exactly the "split coordinate space" bug
   class the Phase-2 ledger watch-list warns about. Composed directly from a
   plain <see cref="ContextMenu.ContextMenuContext"/> CascadingValue (no
   wrapper element at all) + the reusable <c>ContextMenuContent</c> building
   block instead — a SIBLING of the root div above, not nested inside it, so
   it can never affect that div's own layout/positioning context either.
   Right-click DETECTION lives entirely in gantt-v3.js's own delegated
   'contextmenu' listener (see GanttV3RegisterBarContextMenuAsync's own
   remarks) — never a Blazor-bound @@oncontextmenu on any individual
   (virtualized) bar, which would cost one extra event binding per rendered
   bar for a feature most charts never opt into. *@
@if (BarContextMenu is not null)
{
    <CascadingValue Value="@_barContextMenuContext" IsFixed="false">
        <ContextMenuContent>
            @if (_barContextMenuTask is not null)
            {
                @BarContextMenu(_barContextMenuTask)
            }
        </ContextMenuContent>
    </CascadingValue>
}

